<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean Étienne Championnet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1762">1762</a>–<a href="/years/1800">1800</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean Étienne Championnet was a French general of the Revolutionary Wars. Commanding the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, he defeated the Neapolitan attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in late 1798, and in January 1799 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> after fierce street fighting against the lazzaroni, supporting the proclamation of the Parthenopean Republic. Recalled after quarrels with the Directory's commissioners, he died of illness in 1800.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-etienne-championnet.jpg" alt="Jean Étienne Championnet" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Sébastien Rouillard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%A9n%C3%A9ral_JEAN_ETIENNE_CHAMPIONNET.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jean-Étienne Vachier Championnet was born on 13 April 1762. He joined the French Army at an early age and served in the Great Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> he took a prominent part in the movement, and the men of a battalion elected him their commander. In May 1793 he was sent to suppress civil disturbances in the Jura, which he quelled without bloodshed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under Charles Pichegru he served as a brigade commander in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> campaign of 1793 and won the praise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazare-hoche">Lazare Hoche</a> at Weissenburg and in the Palatinate. At the Battle of Fleurus his stubborn fighting in the centre contributed greatly to Jourdan's victory. He then commanded the left wing of the French armies on the Rhine between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuwied">Neuwied</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>, and briefly led the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambre">Sambre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> from 24 to 31 January 1797.</p><p>The Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he took over in 1798 was nominally 32,000 strong but had scarcely 8000 effective soldiers and fifteen cartridges a man, while the Neapolitan army under the Austrian general Karl Mack von Leiberich had a tenfold superiority in numbers. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> his intolerance of opposition, his looting and his unwillingness to curb his troops' atrocities set the population against him. After a quarrel with Guillaume-Charles Faipoult, a representative on mission, he was relieved, accused of graft and briefly imprisoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1799 he commanded the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, at first a force on paper only, which took three months of work to bring into the field. When Barthélemy Catherine Joubert was killed at the Battle of Novi, Championnet took over the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, but the campaign that followed failed throughout. Worn out, he died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antibes">Antibes</a> on 9 January 1800.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> judged him brave, zealous and devoted, a good general of division but an indifferent commander-in-chief. A statue was raised to him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valence">Valence</a> in 1848, and his name is inscribed on the Arc de Triomphe. At the carnival of Frosinone, once part of the Parthenopaean Republic, a puppet of the general is carried through the streets and burned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jean-Étienne Championnet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>French troops under General <b>Championnet</b> fought their way into Naples, and in January 1799 local patriots proclaimed a republic named after Parthenope, the ancient Greek name for the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/parthenopean-republic">Parthenopean Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinois">Valence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antibes">Antibes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.93,4.89&amp;year=1786" aria-label="Valence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jean Étienne Championnet"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5296 3731h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5296 3731h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.93,4.89&amp;year=1786">What was here in 1786? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
